'''Figure Technology Solutions, Inc.''' is a [[financial technology]] company in [[Reno, Nevada]], United States. Established in 2018, it develops and operates [[blockchain]]-based platforms used in lending, capital markets, and [[asset management]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Wolak |first=Sarah |title=Figure expands Intellidebt offerings for debt consolidation |url=https://www.housingwire.com/articles/figure-expands-intellidebt-offerings-for-debt-consolidation/ |access-date=2025-09-11 |website=HousingWire |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Clow |first=Chris |title=Longbridge goes beyond reverse with new 'HELOC for Seniors' |url=https://www.housingwire.com/articles/longbridge-financial-figure-technologies-heloc-for-seniors-reverse-mortgage-alternative/ |access-date=2025-09-11 |website=HousingWire |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":3" />

The company provides consumer and institutional lending products, including HELOCs, [[Cash out refinancing|cash-out refinance]], DSCR loans, and crypto-backed loans, as well as a consumer credit marketplace, Figure Connect.<ref name=":4" />

{{Short description|Set of people living in a particular area or interbreeding}}
{{broader|Population (biology)}}{{other uses|Population (disambiguation)}}
{{Redirect|Human population|the human population of the whole world|World population}}

A '''human population''' is the set of [[Human|people]] living in a particular [[region|geographic area]], increasing or decreasing in [[Population size|size]] over time,<ref name=":0">{{Britannica|id=470303|title=Population|author=Michael S. Teitelbaum}}</ref><ref name=":2" /> but in [[human biology]], a human population may be defined more precisely as "a group of individuals who are more likely to [[mating|mate]] among themselves than among others",<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Miller |first1=Elmer S. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TJAgAQAAMAAJ&q=%22as+a+group+of+individuals+who+are+more%22 |title=Introduction to Anthropology |last2=Weitz |first2=Charles A. |date=1979 |publisher=Prentice-Hall |isbn=978-0-13-478008-5 |pages=182 |language=en}}</ref> which is influenced by a number of social and cultural factors.<ref name=":2" /> Thus, a larger ''geographic population'' can be considered as made of smaller ''breeding populations'' that coexist together,<ref name=":2" /> but in practice, a population is usually defined by a geographic area,<ref>{{Cite Q|Q138201602}}</ref> which can be based on a [[Settlement hierarchy|settlement]], or larger territories (a country, region, city, or the [[World population|whole world]]).<ref name=":2">{{Cite book |last=Relethford |first=John H. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=AuSvIa2xNHQC&pg=PA18 |title=Human Population Genetics |date=2012-03-27 |publisher=John Wiley & Sons |isbn=978-0-470-46467-0 |pages=18 |language=en |quote=geography is a usual starting point in defining a human population}}</ref>

[[Population genetics]] studies differences between populations and differences inside a population. 99.9% of the human genome remains the same across all human populations, but the other genes can be different.<ref name=":3" /> Populations tend to have different [[phenotypic trait]]s based on their geographical location<ref name=":3" /> and so may carry varying gene pools,<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Serrano Sanchez |first=C. |date=1996-04-01 |title=Introduction: The concept of population |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/226825761 |journal=International Journal of Anthropology |language=en |volume=11 |issue=2 |pages=15–18 |doi=10.1007/BF02441407 |issn=1824-3096|doi-access=free }}</ref> but physical traits are not often used to define a population.<ref name=":3" /> Aside from the term "population", humans can also be grouped as [[Race (human categorization)|races]] and [[ethnic groups]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Manglik |first=Mr Rohit |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=CFAzEQAAQBAJ&pg=PA14 |title=Biological Diversity in Human Population: [9789368751571] |date=2023-11-23 |publisher=EduGorilla Publication |isbn=978-93-6875-157-1 |pages=14 |language=en}}</ref>

[[Demography]] is the [[statistical]] study of human populations.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":3" /> Additional criteria may be used to define a demographic population or subpopulation, such as sex and age, as in the female population over 60 years of age. [[Demographic statistics]] may involve the structure<ref name=":4" /> (like [[Population pyramid|age and sex distribution]]),<ref name=":0" /> [[Species distribution|spatial distribution]], and size of a population, or involve [[population dynamics]] (the change of the size and age of a population).<ref name=":4">{{Cite book |last=Carey |first=James R. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=FL2mPHUhrvcC&pg=PA3 |title=Applied Demography for Biologists: with Special Emphasis on Insects |date=1993-02-04 |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=978-0-19-802314-2 |pages=3–4 |language=en}}</ref> However, demographers use the term "population" differently from biologists, even if there are some similarities between these definitions: biologists study the number of individuals in a particular area and its evolution over time under varying conditions, while demographers count individuals by some certain criteria at a given time in a specific region.<ref name=":1">{{Cite book |last=Petersen |first=William |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=PiIxDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA1 |title=From Birth to Death: A Consumer's Guide to Population Studies |date=2017-07-05 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-351-51888-8 |pages=1 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=RSZkrxT9aAYC&pg=PA826 |title=Comprehensive Biology XII |publisher=Firewall Media |isbn=978-81-7008-754-0 |pages=826 |language=en}}</ref>

