{{Short description|Concept involving selective reactions}}
{{other|Orthogonality}}
[[File:Fmoc removal.png|thumb|[[Fmoc]] removal requires [[Base (chemistry)|base]].]]
[[File:Boc eemaldamine.png|thumb|[[Boc]] removal requires [[acid]].]]

In [[chemistry]], '''orthogonality-originating from the Greek ortho(straight) and gonia(straight)-''' is when two or more [[protecting group]]s in a [[chemical compound]] can be removed under conditions that do not affect the other(s)<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Fisher |first=Jed F. |date=2025-03-25 |title=Convergence of the Orthogonal: A Tribute to the Imaginative Chemistry of George Barany |url=https://doi.org/10.1007/s10989-025-10713-2 |journal=International Journal of Peptide Research and Therapeutics |volume=31 |issue=3 |pages=54 |doi=10.1007/s10989-025-10713-2 |issn=1573-3904}}</ref>. Two such protecting groups are said to be orthogonal.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Fisher |first=Jed F. |date=2025-03-25 |title=Convergence of the Orthogonal: A Tribute to the Imaginative Chemistry of George Barany |url=https://doi.org/10.1007/s10989-025-10713-2 |journal=International Journal of Peptide Research and Therapeutics |volume=31 |issue=3 |pages=54 |doi=10.1007/s10989-025-10713-2 |issn=1573-3904}}</ref> A classic example is the [[Fmoc]] group which requires [[Base (chemistry)|base]] to be removed, and the [[Boc]] group which requires [[acid]]; treatment with base will not affect the Boc groups, and treatment with acid will not affect the Fmoc groups.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Fisher |first=Jed F. |date=2025-03-25 |title=Convergence of the Orthogonal: A Tribute to the Imaginative Chemistry of George Barany |url=https://doi.org/10.1007/s10989-025-10713-2 |journal=International Journal of Peptide Research and Therapeutics |volume=31 |issue=3 |pages=54 |doi=10.1007/s10989-025-10713-2 |issn=1573-3904}}</ref>

Orthogonal protection is widely used in [[organic chemistry]] and [[synthetic chemistry]] to allow chemists to perform multiple transformations on complex molecules while controlling the order in which functional groups are revealed.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Cerqueira |first1=Tiago F. T. |last2=Wang |first2=Haichen |last3=Botti |first3=Silvana |last4=Marques |first4=Miguel A. L. |date=2026-01-15 |title=A non-orthogonal representation for materials based on chemical similarity |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41524-025-01916-8 |journal=npj Computational Materials |language=en |volume=12 |issue=1 |pages=48 |doi=10.1038/s41524-025-01916-8 |issn=2057-3960|doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hroncich |first=Caroline |date=2026-08-01 |title=What Matters Most for LC×LC Orthogonality? A Systematic Look at pH, Modes, and Modifiers {{!}} LCGC International |url=https://www.chromatographyonline.com/view/lcxlc-orthogonality-ph-modes-modifiers |access-date=2026-08-01 |website=www.chromatographyonline.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Bishop |first=David M. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=l4zv4dukBT0C&dq=Orthogonality+(chemistry)&pg=PA117 |title=Group Theory and Chemistry |date=1993-01-14 |publisher=Courier Corporation |isbn=978-0-486-67355-4 |language=en}}</ref>
